					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "EXAMPLE 3: OCTAVES & DENSITY\n\nThe easiest way to think of octaves are as layers in Resolume or Photoshop. \nIn the previous example we mentioned that each pixel gets a value.\nThe octave parameter determines how many pixel values are layed on top of each other and how much they affect their neighbors. \nThe best way to see this is to lower the density parameter to somewhere between 0 and 2 and play with the octave parameter.  \nYou can see more detail appearing as you crank up the octaves.\n\nTheoretically the Density adjusts the overall frequency of the Fractal Noise. \nPractically speaking it determines how far you “zoom out”."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 475.742706298828125,
					"width": 896.81512451171875,
