			"119": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "EXAMPLE 4: CHANNELS & OTHERS\n\nRemember that we made noise, modulated it and layered it on top of each other. \nWe did that for just one channel. \nIncreasing the channels will do the entire process again for other color channels. \nBy doing this we get layers of Fractal noise on top of each other for more complex images.\n\nKeep in mind that increasing the channels doubles or triples the computational power required to generate the fractal noise. \n3 channels times 6 octaves is 18 octaves computed each frame.\n\nThe Frequency slider increases the \"noisiness\" of the image.\nThe Gain parameter is a multiplier for each pixel value.\nClamp makes sure that the pixel values do not exceed 1, which can be relevant for higher-bit depths."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 678.65289306640625,
					"width": 896.81512451171875,
					"x": 715.2620849609375,
